{"qid":"Q668412","names":["Pala Empire"],"title":"Pala Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Pala Empire was founded c. 750 CE when Gopala, elected king by Bengali nobles after a period of political disorder, established a Buddhist dynastic state centered in Bihar and Bengal. Under Dharmapala (r. c. 775–810) and Devapala (r. c. 810–850) the empire reached its greatest extent, exercising suzerainty over much of the Gangetic plain and patronizing major Buddhist institutions including Nalanda and Vikramashila monasteries, which attracted scholars from Tibet, China, and Southeast Asia. Pala power declined after the mid-ninth century as the Gurjara-Pratiharas, Rashtrakutas, and later the Chola naval expedition of 1025 eroded their influence; the last significant Pala ruler, Ramapala (r. c. 1077–1120), briefly recovered Bengal before the dynasty collapsed under Sena pressure by the mid-twelfth century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territories in Bihar and Bengal are attested by copper-plate grants, stone inscriptions, and pilgrimage accounts; the empire's fluctuating suzerainty over Kanauj and the upper Gangetic region is reconstructed from political claims in epigraphic records, and frontier extents are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Pala dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Pala-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Pala Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pala_Empire"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Pala Empire","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Gopala I","nativeName":"গোপাল","roman":"Gopāla I","start":750,"end":774,"image":null,"imageUrl":nu